 Authorization\n258\t\n259\t| Endpoint | Methods | Auth Mechanism | Enforcement Point | Policy |\n260\t|--------|-------|--------------|-----------------|------|\n261\t| Tracking Server API | All | kubernetes-auth plugin (Kubernetes service account bearer token) | Flask application via mlflow-kubernetes-plugins entry point | RBAC with role-based per-resource permissions |\n262\t\n263\t### Security Evidence\n264\t\n265\t| Kind | Target | Detail | Signal Type |\n266\t|----|------|------|-----------|\n267\t| tls-config | cryptography | TLS/cryptography library dependency | dependency-signal |\n268\t| rbac-ref | kubernetes | Kubernetes client library (RBAC capable) | dependency-signal |\n269\t\n270\t### FIPS Compliance\n271\t\n272\tThis is a Python application; Go-specific check-payload validations (CGO_ENABLED, strictfipsruntime, dynamic linking) do not apply.\n273\t\n274\t#### Application-Level Crypto\n275\t\n276\t| Aspect | Value | Source |\n277\t|--------|-------|--------|\n278\t| **TLS configuration** | Delegates to Python `cryptography` library (uses OpenSSL when available on UBI9 base) | pyproject.toml:37 |\n279\t| **Crypto libraries** | `cryptography` (<49,>=43.0.0) — uses OpenSSL backend on UBI9 | pyproject.toml:37 |\n280\t| **Certificate handling** | Unknown — no explicit certificate configuration detected in server entrypoint | Dockerfile.konflux:80 |\n281\t| **Non-FIPS crypto risks** | Not verified — no explicit FIPS mode activation (no OPENSSL_FIPS env var or fips=yes configuration) | Dockerfile.konflux:38-43 |\n282\t\n283\tThe UBI9-minimal base image includes OpenSSL which supports FIPS mode when the host system enables it, but the MLflow container does not explicitly configure or enforce FIPS mode. The Python `cryptography` package (>=43.0.0) uses the OpenSSL backend by default on Linux, inheriting the host's FIPS policy when available. No explicit FIPS opt-in or opt-out was detected. [source: Dockerfile.konflux:37, pyproject.toml:37]\n284\t## Data Flows\n285\t\n286\t- **Entry and service surface:** Clients connect to the MLflow Tracking Server on port 5000. The `kubernetes-auth` plugin intercepts inbound requests and validates Kubernetes service account bearer tokens from the `Authorization` header. When workspaces are enabled, the `X-MLFLOW-WORKSPACE` header routes requests to the appropriate namespace-scoped workspace. The Flask application dispatches authenticated requests to tracking, model registry, artifact, gateway proxy, assistant, job, and MCP server API handlers. The `mlflow-skinny` service identity exposes the same surface with a minimal dependency set. [source: Dockerfile.konflux:76-80, mlflow/server/__init__.py:68-88, mlflow/tracking/request_auth/kubernetes_request_auth_provider.py:229-258]\n287\t- **Runtime inventory:** The container runs gunicorn or uvicorn as the ASGI/WSGI server, hosting the Flask-based MLflow tracking server. The 6 runtime components form a layered stack: the container entrypoint invokes `mlflow server`, which initializes the Flask app with security middleware, endpoint routing, workspace handlers, and optional Prometheus metrics export. The mlflow-tracing SDK is packaged alongside but operates as a client-side instrumentation library. [source: Dockerfile.konflux:80, mlflow/server/__init__.py:68-96]\n288\t- **Downstream interactions:** The tracking server stores experiment metadata in a SQL backend (PostgreSQL via psycopg2, or SQLite) and manages artifacts via a pluggable store that can target S3-compatible storage (boto3), Google Cloud Storage, or local filesystem. The Kubernetes API is used for workspace management (`kubernetes://` store URI) and optionally for project execution as Kubernetes Jobs. External LLM provider integrations (OpenAI, AWS Bedrock) are available in the codebase but the AI Gateway feature is disabled by default in RHOAI (`MLFLOW_ENABLE_AI_GATEWAY=false`). [source: Dockerfile.konflux:38-43, 50, mlflow/projects/kubernetes.py:8-9, 32-49, mlflow/gateway/providers/bedrock.py:212, mlflow/deployments/openai/__init__.py:175]\n289\t- **Security context:** The `kubernetes-auth` plugin and RBAC module enforce authentication and authorization across all tracking server endpoints. 19 environment-variable secrets reference API keys for external LLM providers (OpenAI, Anthropic, Cohere, Mistral, etc.) and cloud storage credentials. The `cryptography` package (>=43.0.0) provides TLS support via OpenSSL on the UBI9 base image. [source: mlflow/server/auth/__init__.py:229-367, mlflow/tracking/request_auth/kubernetes_request_auth_provider.py:38-41, pyproject.toml:37]\n290\t\n291\t## Integration Points\n292\t\n293\t- **AWS (S3-compatible storage):** Python SDK client; role: runtime-integration; protocol: HTTPS; port: Configured by runtime; purpose: AWS service operations via boto3. [source: libs/skinny/pyproject.toml:2, 26, 28-47, 59, 61-66, 68, 70, 72, 74, 79-80, 82, 92-96, 98-99, mlflow/deployments/openai/__init__.py:175, mlflow/gateway/app.py:301, 305, 315, 419, 438, 457, mlflow/gateway/providers/bedrock.py:212]\n294\t- **Google Cloud Storage:** Python SDK client; role: runtime-integration; protocol: HTTPS; port: Configured by runtime; purpose: GCS operations via Python SDK. [source: libs/skinny/pyproject.toml:2, 26, 28-47, 59, 61-66, 68, 70, 72, 74, 79-80, 82, 92-96, 98-99, mlflow/deployments/openai/__init__.py:175, mlflow/gateway/app.py:301, 305, 315, 419, 438, 457, mlflow/gateway/providers/bedrock.py:212]\n295\t- **Kubernetes API:** Python client library; purpose: Kubernetes resource operations via Python SDK. [source: libs/skinny/pyproject.toml:2, 26, 28-47, 59, 61-66, 68, 70, 72, 74, 79-80, 82, 92-96, 98-99, mlflow/deployments/openai/__init__.py:175, mlflow/gateway/app.py:301, 305, 315, 419, 438, 457, mlflow/gateway/providers/bedrock.py:212]\n296\t- **api.openai.com:** HTTP client; protocol: HTTPS; port: 443; purpose: Literal outbound HTTP endpoint. [source: libs/skinny/pyproject.toml:2, 26, 28-47, 59, 61-66, 68, 70, 72, 74, 79-80