{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-65e0cd68-7b0f-4087-9d29-30f42ab2b717","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-9269d8050c529411","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8009-r33s780v/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8009-decomposition.md","content":"# Decomposition Summary: RHAISTRAT-8009 — Workspace-aware artifact serving for multi-tenant MLflow deployments\n\nStrategy status: In Progress, priority Critical, all work within the MLflow Team's components\n(MLflow, MLflow Operator, mlflow-kubernetes-plugins). Effort estimate M-L (4-7 sprints).\n\n## Triage Outcome\n\nFull decomposition (not below-threshold, not docs-only): the strategy spans three affected\ncomponents, is estimated M-L (4-7 sprints), and contains genuine unknowns (open questions,\n\"needs validation\" assumptions, and pending design decisions) whose answers change which epics\nexist or what they do.\n\n## Staff Engineer / SME Input Overrides\n\nThe Staff Engineer / SME Input section is non-empty and takes precedence over the AI-generated\ntechnical approach. Three overrides were applied and are reflected in the epic scopes:\n\n1. **No new flag — combine existing flags.** The originally proposed\n   `--artifacts-only-workspace-aware` server flag and `spec.artifactsOnly.workspaceAware` CR\n   field are removed from scope. The server implementation epic composes the existing\n   `--artifacts-only` and `--enable-workspaces` flags instead; no epic introduces a new server\n   flag or CR field.\n2. **Operator work is a split deployment, not a config toggle.** The operator epic deploys a\n   dedicated artifacts-only MLflow instance as a separate Deployment with its own Service and\n   HTTPRoute and configures the main tracking deployment to route artifact traffic to it —\n   not a single-deployment configuration flag.\n3. **SSAR detail de-specified.** Epic scopes state that workspace authorization reuses the\n   existing auth plugin infrastructure without dictating Kubernetes API mechanics\n   (SelfSubjectAccessReview against `mlflow.kubeflow.org` pseudo-resources is an\n   mlflow-kubernetes-plugins implementation detail). The kube-rbac-proxy sidecar and\n   `--app-name kubernetes-auth` setup are unchanged.\n\n## Component Mappings\n\nCanonical Jira components come from `.context/rhai-components.txt`. Two of the strategy's\naffected components have no canonical Jira component of their own; the closest canonical name\nis `MLflow` in both cases, and every epic therefore carries `component: MLflow`:\n\n| Strategy component | Canonical component | Note |\n|----|----|----|\n| MLflow | MLflow | Verbatim match |\n| MLflow Operator | MLflow | No canonical \"MLflow Operator\" component exists; the operator manages the MLflow component and is developed by the same team — logged span, not an invented name |\n| mlflow-kubernetes-plugins | MLflow | No canonical component; the plugin is the MLflow server's auth/workspace provider, consumed by the MLflow image build — logged span, not an invented name |\n\n## Epic List\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E001 | Workspace resolution feasibility in the artifacts-only context (fail-mode and authorization-bypass investigation) | Investigation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E002 | Artifact traffic routing design for the split-server topology | Investigation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E003 | Audit telemetry channel for workspace access denials | Investigation | MLflow Team | P2 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 | mlflow-kubernetes-plugins: workspace resolution in the artifacts-only context | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E005 | MLflow server: compose `--artifacts-only` with `--enable-workspaces` for workspace-validated artifact serving | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E006 | MLflow operator: split-server topology for the dedicated artifacts-only instance | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E007 | Audit logging for workspace access denials in artifact serving | Implementation | MLflow Team | P2 |\n\nBranch epic (conditional, not a main-plan node — see Conditional Branches):\nRHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E008 — MLflow tracking server artifact delegation configuration (P1),\nexists only if the routing-design investigation selects server-side delegation.\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[\"E001 Workspace resolution feasibility (Inv, P0)\"] --> E004[\"E004 Plugin workspace resolution (Impl, P0)\"]\n    E001 --> E005[\"E005 Server flag composition + validation (Impl, P0)\"]\n    E004 --> E005\n    E002[\"E002 Artifact traffic routing design (Inv, P1)\"] --> E006[\"E006 Operator split-server topology (Impl, P1)\"]\n    E002 -.-> E008B[\"BRANCH-B-E008 Server delegation config (branch, P1)\"]\n    E003[\"E003 Audit telemetry channel (Inv, P2)\"] --> E007[\"E007 Audit logging (Impl, P2)\"]\n    E005 --> E007\n```\n\nRoots: E001, E002, E003 (three independent investigations). Critical path:\nE001 → E004 → E005 → E007 (4 epics).\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → E004 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The investigation determines whether plugin workspace resolution / workspace store initialization is coupled to tracking store initialization — i.e. whether the plugin epic is verification-only or a refactor. True gate (`rewrite`). |\n| E001 → E005 | Rule 3 | The investigation validates artifact path → experiment resolvability (the primary authorization-bypass vector) and enumerates failure modes; outcomes change the server validation approach. True gate (`rewrite`). |\n| E004 → E005 | Rule 13 (library → consumer) | The server's workspace validation path builds against the plugin's workspace resolution interface (the `mlflow.workspace_provider` entry point / workspace store abstraction); the server epic consumes the extended plugin as a library. |\n| E002 → E006 | Rule 3 | The routing design determines the HTTPRoute design and the topology configuration; the operator epic's scope is decided by the outcome. True gate (`rewrite`). |\n| E002 → BRANCH-B-E008 | Rule 3 + conditional branch | Server-side delegation work exists only in outcome B of the routing investigation (bounded outcomes ≤ 3: ingress-level routing only vs. server-side delegation config). |\n| E003 → E007 | Rule 3 | The telemetry-channel decision (server log vs. OTLP vs. Kubernetes audit log) determines the audit implementation's scope. True gate (`rewrite`). |\n| E005 → E007 | Rule 12/13 (built-on surface) | Audit hooks instrument the workspace validation path in the server's artifact request handling — the audit epic builds on the validation code the server epic produces; serial on the same surface. |\n\nDeliberate non-edges (parallelism):\n\n- **E005 ∥ E006**: different repos (MLflow server vs. mlflow-operator). The operator's chart\n  templates reference the flag combination as configuration, not as an API/CRD/library the\n  operator code builds against — the coupling is expressed as an acceptance criterion on the\n  operator epic (\"reconciliation validated against an MLflow image supporting the combined\n  flags\"), never as a DAG edge.\n- **E001 ∥ E002 ∥ E003**: the three investigations resolve independent unknowns (workspace\n  resolution feasibility, routing design, audit channel) and start in parallel.\n- **E004 ∥ E006, E005 ∥ BRANCH-B-E008**: different repos / independent surfaces, no shared\n  build artifact.\n\nNo onboarding chain is generated: all three strategy components already exist and ship\n(MLflow and mlflow-operator have Konflux builds per architecture context; the plugin is\npublished on PyPI and consumed by the MLflow image build) — see Health Warnings for the\nplugin caveat.\n\n## Open-Question Triage (Step 3)\n\nAll unknowns were collected from the open questions table, risks, and \"needs validation\"\nassumptions before creating investigations:\n\n| Unknown | Source | Disposition |\n|---------|--------|-------------|\n| Does workspace store / plugin initialization depend on tracking store availability? | Open question 3, assumption, risk | **Investigation E001** — determines verify-only vs. refactor scope of the plugin epic and the server's init approach. |\n| Do artifact request paths resolve to experiments (path ambiguity across backends)? | Assumption, risk 1 | **Investigation E001** — same experiment and deliverable (exercise workspace resolution in the artifacts-only context); also the fail-mode / authorization-bypass validation for this tenant-isolation strategy (Rule 19). |\n| Failure modes of workspace-aware artifact serving (store unreachable, unresolvable path, malformed context) | NFR, risks | **Investigation E001** — enumerated with fail-closed handling; combined because the same investigation produces the failure-mode spec. |\n| How should the operator configure artifact traffic routing to the dedicated instance? | Open question 2 | **Investigation E002** — bounded outcomes (ingress-level routing vs. server-side delegation); gates the operator epic and conditionally adds BRANCH-B-E008. |\n| Which telemetry channel for workspace access denials? | Open question 4 | **Investigation E003** — a PM/Security decision that determines the audit implementation's scope; gates E007. |\n| Acceptable latency overhead per artifact request (caching at launch vs. follow-up)? | Open question 1, NFR, risk 4 | **AC on E005** — the answer cannot precede the implementation (it requires measuring the built validation path), and the implementation proceeds the same way regardless; the epic measures and documents overhead and records the caching decision. If measurement shows unacceptable overhead, a follow-up short-TTL caching epic is required (see Health Warnings). |\n| Flags composable without breaking individual-flag initialization | Assumption 1 | **AC on E005** — the server epic's own work; failure = fix-and-retry in the same epic. |\n| Single-tenant deployments do not set `--enable-workspaces` | Assumption 3 | **AC on E005** (backwards-compatibility tests) — covered by HLR-4. |\n| Operator Helm chart supports a second Deployment without structural changes | Assumption 4 | **AC on E006** — the operator epic's own work; the Helm-in-operator pattern is corroborated by architecture context. |\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Requirement | Epic(s) | Notes |\n|-----|----------|-------------|---------|-------|\n| HLR-1 | P0 | `--artifacts-only` combinable with `--enable-workspaces` and `--workspace-store-uri` | RHAISTRAT-8009-E005 | Flag composition, workspace-provider-only initialization, non-artifact APIs disabled. |\n| HLR-2 | P0 | Artifact requests validated against workspace membership; non-members denied HTTP 403 | RHAISTRAT-8009-E004, RHAISTRAT-8009-E005 | Enforcement in the server's artifact request handling; resolution supplied by the plugin (E004 feeds E005, Rule 13). |\n| HLR-3 | P0 | Fail-closed: workspace store unreachable → requests denied | RHAISTRAT-8009-E005 | Failure-mode spec produced by E001. |\n| HLR-4 | P0 | Single-tenant / non-workspace deployments retain current behavior | RHAISTRAT-8009-E005 | Backwards-compatibility ACs; the operator epic additionally carries a no-split-config → identical reconciliation AC. |\n| HLR-5 | P1 | Operator deploys dedicated artifacts-only Deployment + Service + HTTPRoute | RHAISTRAT-8009-E006 | |\n| HLR-6 | P1 | Main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated instance | RHAISTRAT-8009-E006 (+ RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E008 in branch B) | Routing mechanism decided by E002. |\n| HLR-7 | P1 | Auth plugin workspace resolution works in artifacts-only context without tracking store initialization | RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 (bundled) | Bundled with the P0 work: HLR-7 is the plugin-side enabler of the P0 workspace validation (HLR-2) — it edits the same workspace-resolution code path in mlflow-kubernetes-plugins that the P0 validation depends on, so a split would double-touch the same plugin surface and create two epics building the same resolution path. The epic's P0 priority comes from inheritance (it gates all P0 server work), not from the P1 label. |\n| HLR-8 | P2 | Audit logging for workspace access denials through server logs | RHAISTRAT-8009-E007 | Channel decision (E003) may extend scope beyond the server-log baseline. |\n\nAll 8 HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Planning note (epic size)**: the operator split-server topology epic (E006) is 2-3 sprints\n  by the strategy's own effort estimate — above the ~2-week guideline. Kept as one logical\n  change: the split topology is a single coherent operator change (new Helm chart templates\n  for the artifacts-only Deployment/Service/HTTPRoute, reconciler wiring, workspace config\n  propagation, CR validation); a split would fragment the same Helm chart and reconciler\n  surface. **Re-split trigger**: re-split this epic if the chart-template work exceeds the\n  estimate — split \"artifacts-only Deployment/Service/HTTPRoute templates\" from \"main\n  deployment artifact-routing configuration\" if the two grow independently.\n- **Planning note (epic size)**: the server flag-composition epic (E005) is 1-2 sprints by the\n  strategy's estimate — at or above the upper bound of the guideline. Kept as one logical\n  change: flag composition, workspace-provider initialization, and per-request validation are\n  one mechanical pass over the same server initialization and artifact request handling code.\n  **Re-split trigger**: re-split if the workspace validation wiring in the artifact proxy\n  proves independently schedulable from the flag/init changes, or if the audit finds materially\n  more artifact request paths than the strategy's estimate.\n- **mlflow-kubernetes-plugins has no architecture-context file**: it is consumed as an external\n  PyPI package (v1.5.0) by the MLflow image build (per mlflow.md), and the strategy classifies\n  it as internal/existing — so no onboarding chain was generated. Caveat: the plugin's source\n  repository and build/release pipeline are not verified by architecture context. If the source\n  is not modifiable by the team, add an onboarding chain (repo-onboarding + license-validation →\n  konflux-onboarding) before the plugin epic and re-plan its repo-access assumptions.\n- **Deferred product decision (latency/caching)**: no latency target exists in the RFE or\n  architecture context. The server epic measures per-request overhead and documents it; the\n  at-launch-vs-follow-up caching decision is recorded with PM/Engineering. If the measurement\n  shows unacceptable overhead, a follow-up short-TTL caching epic is required (the strategy's\n  risk mitigation already sketches the caching approach).\n- **Strategy-level process gates pending**: Architecture Review, ODH/RHOAI Build Onboarding,\n  Licence Validation, and other prerequisites are \"Pending human review\" at the strategy level.\n  No epic-specific onboarding is needed (existing components already build via Konflux), but\n  the split-server topology design should clear the pending architecture review before the\n  operator epic's design is finalized.\n- **Rule 19 handling (safety-critical strategy)**: this strategy is a tenant-isolation\n  (authorization-boundary) change. E001 serves as the fail-mode and authorization-bypass\n  investigation (artifact path resolution ambiguity is the primary bypass vector) and blocks\n  the main implementation. E003 covers the security audit/telemetry decision; it gates only\n  the P2 audit epic because the channel choice does not change what the main implementation\n  builds.\n- **Documentation Support is \"Pending human review\"**, not confirmed: no docs-authoring epic is\n  generated. If documentation support is confirmed, add a docs-authoring epic blocked by all\n  implementation epics (Rule 11).\n- **Design doc / ADR \"to be created\"**: the routing-design investigation (E002) produces the\n  routing decision record; if an ADR is required for the split-server topology, it falls out\n  of that investigation's deliverable and the pending architecture review.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applicable. All dependencies are internal and existing (workspace store, auth plugin,\nGateway API, kube-rbac-proxy); there is no external dependency with uncertain timing, so no\nTier 1 / Tier 2 split is needed.\n\n## Conditional Branches\n\nThe routing-design investigation (E002) has bounded outcomes (≤ 3) that change downstream\nstructure:\n\n- **Outcome A — ingress-level routing only**: artifact traffic is routed to the dedicated\n  artifacts-only instance entirely at the HTTPRoute/ingress layer; the tracking server needs\n  no configuration change. No additional epic; the operator epic proceeds as specified.\n- **Outcome B — server-side delegation required**: the tracking server needs a configuration\n  change to delegate artifact operations to the dedicated instance. Branch epic\n  RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E008 (MLflow tracking server artifact delegation configuration,\n  P1, component MLflow) is selected; the operator epic's scope is correspondingly rewritten\n  to propagate the delegation configuration.\n\nBranch epics are plan documents attached to the gating investigation, not standalone Jira\nissues; they are excluded from the main-plan critical path. `epic_count` counts the 7\nmain-plan epics; including the branch epic the total is 8.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":71144,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-92d484210dda7c67","agent_id":"acf985de57b4e149a","session_id":"b73c52d2-9810-4f0f-a474-9e7932db2044","uuid":"fcc0ec48-009b-4f45-927a-c739e1174e83","timestamp":"2026-