{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-7e6781e3-8e05-4865-8b61-2dae4dcfb6a2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-922685e2e0773b6b","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8009-r33s780v/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8009-E001.md","content":"# Workspace resolution feasibility in the artifacts-only context (fail-mode and authorization-bypass investigation)\n\n## Description\n\nThis investigation resolves whether MLflow's workspace resolution can function in an\nartifacts-only server context — where tracking stores and model registry stores are not\ninitialized — before the implementation work is committed to a specific approach. It is the\nfail-mode and authorization-bypass investigation for this tenant-isolation strategy: it\nvalidates that artifact request paths can be resolved to their parent experiment's workspace\n(the primary bypass vector if resolution is ambiguous) and enumerates the failure modes the\nworkspace-aware artifacts-only server must handle fail-closed. The findings determine whether\nthe auth plugin's workspace resolution is verification-only or requires refactoring into an\nindependent initialization path, and they shape the server's workspace validation approach.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Map the initialization dependencies of workspace resolution in the `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins`\n  auth plugin and the `kubernetes://` workspace store: determine whether initialization is\n  coupled to tracking store or model registry store initialization, with cited code paths\n  (the plugin is registered through the `mlflow.workspace_provider` entry point group and\n  loaded by `WorkspaceStoreRegistry`).\n- Validate that the workspace provider can initialize in an artifacts-only server context\n  (no tracking/registry stores), including the requirement for an explicit\n  `--workspace-store-uri` when workspace-aware mode is active.\n- Validate artifact path resolution: whether artifact request paths\n  (e.g. `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`) carry sufficient information to resolve the\n  parent experiment and its workspace assignment, scoped to the S3 artifact store (the\n  standard RHOAI configuration); document limitations for non-standard artifact roots.\n- Enumerate failure modes of workspace-aware artifact serving — workspace store unreachable,\n  artifact path unresolvable to an experiment, malformed or missing workspace context — and\n  specify the fail-closed handling for each, consistent with the strategy's fail-closed\n  posture (deny rather than serve without validation).\n- Produce a refactoring scope determination for the auth plugin: verification-only versus\n  factoring workspace resolution into an independent initialization path within the plugin.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- The initialization dependency map of workspace resolution (plugin and workspace store versus\n  tracking/registry store initialization) is documented with cited code paths, and a\n  pass/fail determination with evidence is recorded for whether the workspace provider can\n  initialize without tracking stores.\n- A documented determination, with test evidence where reproducible, of whether artifact\n  request paths in the S3 artifact store layout resolve to parent experiments, including\n  custom artifact roots; limitations for non-standard roots are documented.\n- Every enumerated failure mode has a specified fail-closed handling; any case where\n  fail-closed semantics cannot be guaranteed is escalated as a finding that changes the\n  implementation approach.\n- The plugin refactoring scope determination (verify-only vs. refactor, with the specific\n  coupling points to decouple) is recorded with a recommendation.\n- The investigation report is written so the server implementation and plugin work can be\n  planned directly from its findings.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\nThis investigation does not directly cover a High Level Requirement; it gates the epics that\nimplement HLR-2 (workspace validation of artifact requests, P0), HLR-3 (fail-closed behavior,\nP0), and HLR-7 (auth plugin workspace resolution without tracking store initialization, P1),\nand its outcomes determine their approach. The fail-closed semantics and S3 scoping come from\nthe strategy's Non-Functional Requirements (Security) and risk mitigations.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":76219,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-92d484210dda7c67","agent_id":"acf985de57b4e149a","session_id":"b73c52d2-9810-4f0f-a474-9e7932db2044","uuid":"329b7862-7e80-41ad-9972-c0ee5c5be9a5","timestamp":"2026-10-25T20:43:37.218Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8009 into epics"}
