{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-47d57e07-7d09-41bf-bad4-7d4b618f87f9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-9a25deb06ad47e7f","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8009-r33s780v/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8009-E005.md","content":"# MLflow server: compose `--artifacts-only` with `--enable-workspaces` for workspace-validated artifact serving\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic makes the MLflow tracking server's `--artifacts-only` mode composable with the\nexisting `--enable-workspaces` flag, so the artifacts-only server initializes only the\nworkspace provider needed to validate artifact requests against tenant boundaries. No new\nserver flag is introduced: when both existing flags are set, the server requires an explicit\n`--workspace-store-uri`, initializes only the workspace provider, keeps tracking and model\nregistry stores uninitialized, and keeps non-artifact API endpoints disabled. For each\nartifact request, the server resolves the artifact path to its parent experiment, looks up\nthe experiment's workspace assignment via the workspace store, and delegates authorization to\nthe existing auth plugin infrastructure. The design is fail-closed.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Make `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` without breaking the server\n  initialization sequence for either flag used individually; when both are set, require an\n  explicit `--workspace-store-uri` (fail to start with a clear error if it is missing) and\n  initialize only the workspace provider.\n- Keep tracking stores and model registry stores uninitialized and non-artifact API\n  endpoints disabled in the combined mode.\n- Add workspace validation to artifact request handling: resolve the artifact path to its\n  parent experiment (artifacts are stored under experiment-scoped paths, e.g.\n  `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`), look up the experiment's workspace assignment via\n  the workspace store, and delegate authorization to the existing auth plugin\n  infrastructure, building on the plugin's artifacts-only-capable workspace resolution.\n- Fail-closed behavior: if the workspace store is unreachable, artifact requests are denied\n  (not served without validation); requests with invalid or missing workspace context are\n  denied.\n- Backwards compatibility: `--artifacts-only` without `--enable-workspaces` preserves the\n  current artifacts-only behavior exactly; deployments that do not set `--enable-workspaces`\n  are unaffected. Rollback is omitting either flag.\n- Scope the initial implementation to the S3 artifact store (the standard RHOAI\n  configuration) and document limitations for non-standard artifact roots, per the\n  feasibility investigation's findings.\n- Measure per-request latency overhead of workspace validation (workspace store lookup plus\n  auth plugin validation) and document it, recording the caching decision (at-launch vs.\n  follow-up) with PM/Engineering.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A server started with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri\n  kubernetes://` initializes only the workspace provider; tracking and model registry stores\n  remain uninitialized and non-artifact API endpoints remain disabled (verified by test).\n- A server started with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces` but no `--workspace-store-uri`\n  fails to start with a clear error.\n- Given a user who is a member of workspace A requesting an artifact belonging to an\n  experiment in workspace A through the artifacts-only instance, the artifact is served —\n  measured by HTTP 200 with correct artifact content.\n- Given a user who is a member of workspace A but not workspace B requesting an artifact\n  belonging to an experiment in workspace B, the request is denied — measured by HTTP 403\n  with an error message indicating insufficient workspace permissions.\n- Given the workspace-aware artifacts-only instance running with the workspace store\n  (Kubernetes API) unreachable, artifact requests are denied (fail-closed) — measured by\n  HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is unavailable.\n- Given `--artifacts-only` set without `--enable-workspaces`, any authenticated user's\n  artifact request is served without workspace validation — current behavior preserved,\n  measured by HTTP 200 regardless of workspace membership; single-tenant and non-workspace\n  deployments are unaffected.\n- The per-request latency overhead of workspace validation is measured and documented, and\n  the caching decision (implement now vs. follow-up) is recorded.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1 (P0): `--artifacts-only` can be combined with `--enable-workspaces` and\n  `--workspace-store-uri` to enable workspace-validated artifact serving — this epic's\n  primary requirement.\n- HLR-2 (P0): artifact requests are validated against workspace membership; non-members are\n  denied HTTP 403 — enforced in this epic's artifact request handling.\n- HLR-3 (P0): workspace-aware artifact serving is fail-closed when the workspace store is\n  unreachable — this epic's fail-closed behavior.\n- HLR-4 (P0): single-tenant deployments and configurations without `--enable-workspaces`\n  retain current behavior with no changes — this epic's backwards-compatibility scope.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":79326,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-92d484210dda7c67","agent_id":"acf985de57b4e149a","session_id":"b73c52d2-9810-4f0f-a474-9e7932db2044","uuid":"a1d87e39-cee8-4ac1-b39e-f205174f53d1","timestamp":"2026-10-25T20:44:21.847Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8009 into epics"}
