{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-98df23a52947ec97","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8009-E001\n3\ttitle: Workspace resolution feasibility in the artifacts-only context (fail-mode and\n4\t  authorization-bypass investigation)\n5\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\n6\tcomponent: MLflow\n7\tteam: MLflow Team\n8\ttype: Investigation\n9\tpriority: P0\n10\tdependencies: []\n11\tinvestigation_signals:\n12\t  question_specificity: 1\n13\t  source_accessibility: 1\n14\t  local_runnability: 0\n15\t  cluster_hardware_dependence: -1\n16\t  human_judgment_required: 0\n17\timplementation_type: null\n18\tai_signals: null\n19\tai_implementability: Medium\n20\tai_implementability_score: 1\n21\tjira_key: null\n22\tbranch: null\n23\tgated_by: null\n24\tgate_failure_impact: null\n25\t---\n26\t# Workspace resolution feasibility in the artifacts-only context (fail-mode and authorization-bypass investigation)\n27\t\n28\t## Description\n29\t\n30\tThis investigation resolves whether MLflow's workspace resolution can function in an\n31\tartifacts-only server context — where tracking stores and model registry stores are not\n32\tinitialized — before the implementation work is committed to a specific approach. It is the\n33\tfail-mode and authorization-bypass investigation for this tenant-isolation strategy: it\n34\tvalidates that artifact request paths can be resolved to their parent experiment's workspace\n35\t(the primary bypass vector if resolution is ambiguous) and enumerates the failure modes the\n36\tworkspace-aware artifacts-only server must handle fail-closed. The findings determine whether\n37\tthe auth plugin's workspace resolution is verification-only or requires refactoring into an\n38\tindependent initialization path, and they shape the server's workspace validation approach.\n39\t\n40\t## Scope\n41\t\n42\t- Map the initialization dependencies of workspace resolution in the `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins`\n43\t  auth plugin and the `kubernetes://` workspace store: determine whether initialization is\n44\t  coupled to tracking store or model registry store initialization, with cited code paths\n45\t  (the plugin is registered through the `mlflow.workspace_provider` entry point group and\n46\t  loaded by `WorkspaceStoreRegistry`).\n47\t- Validate that the workspace provider can initialize in an artifacts-only server context\n48\t  (no tracking/registry stores), including the requirement for an explicit\n49\t  `--workspace-store-uri` when workspace-aware mode is active.\n50\t- Validate artifact path resolution: whether artifact request paths\n51\t  (e.g. `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`) carry sufficient information to resolve the\n52\t  parent experiment and its workspace assignment, scoped to the S3 artifact store (the\n53\t  standard RHOAI configuration); document limitations for non-standard artifact roots.\n54\t- Enumerate failure modes of workspace-aware artifact serving — workspace store unreachable,\n55\t  artifact path unresolvable to an experiment, malformed or missing workspace context — and\n56\t  specify the fail-closed handling for each, consistent with the strategy's fail-closed\n57\t  posture (deny rather than serve without validation).\n58\t- Produce a refactoring scope determination for the auth plugin: verification-only versus\n59\t  factoring workspace resolution into an independent initialization path within the plugin.\n60\t\n61\t## Acceptance Criteria\n62\t\n63\t- The initialization dependency map of workspace resolution (plugin and workspace store versus\n64\t  tracking/registry store initialization) is documented with cited code paths, and a\n65\t  pass/fail determination with evidence is recorded for whether the workspace provider can\n66\t  initialize without tracking stores.\n67\t- A documented determination, with test evidence where reproducible, of whether artifact\n68\t  request paths in the S3 artifact store layout resolve to parent experiments, including\n69\t  custom artifact roots; limitations for non-standard roots are documented.\n70\t- Every enumerated failure mode has a specified fail-closed handling; any case where\n71\t  fail-closed semantics cannot be guaranteed is escalated as a finding that changes the\n72\t  implementation approach.\n73\t- The plugin refactoring scope determination (verify-only vs. refactor, with the specific\n74\t  coupling points to decouple) is recorded with a recommendation.\n75\t- The investigation report is written so the server implementation and plugin work can be\n76\t  planned directly from its findings.\n77\t\n78\t## HLR Traceability\n79\t\n80\tThis investigation does not directly cover a High Level Requirement; it gates the epics that\n81\timplement HLR-2 (workspace validation of artifact requests, P0), HLR-3 (fail-closed behavior,\n82\tP0), and HLR-7 (auth plugin workspace resolution without tracking store initialization, P1),\n83\tand its outcomes determine their approach. The fail-closed semantics and S3 scoping come from\n84\tthe strategy's Non-Functional Requirements (Security) and risk mitigations.\n85\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b2bd5c6e3d816421","agent_id":"a90bddf3d6b94a5b2","session_id":"b73c52d2-9810-4f0f-a474-9e7932db2044","uuid":"0309bc72-9a8f-404c-ae75-a4e22e4226bf","timestamp":"2026-10-25T20:47:32.337Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-98df23a52947ec97","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8009 decomposition"}
